WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.689
This briefing comes from Book 12 of
the Federal Modernization Workbook,

2
00:00:04.689 --> 00:00:09.792
Security Operations and Telemetry: how
an agency makes every part of its estate

3
00:00:09.792 --> 00:00:14.663
emit evidence, turns it into detection
and response, and proves that it works.

4
00:00:14.663 --> 00:00:18.408
I'll take four questions: why you
can't defend what you can't see,

5
00:00:18.408 --> 00:00:22.004
what each plane must emit and
how findings reach one queue,

6
00:00:22.004 --> 00:00:26.949
who runs security operations, and how to
run detection, response and monitoring.

7
00:00:26.949 --> 00:00:30.847
The short version: design
visibility instead of assuming it,

8
00:00:30.847 --> 00:00:34.960
and close a finding only when
the thing is fixed, with proof.

9
00:00:35.710 --> 00:00:37.822
Why can't you defend what you can't see?

10
00:00:37.822 --> 00:00:40.667
Most agencies run a SIEM,
pay for log storage,

11
00:00:40.667 --> 00:00:43.836
and can show an auditor
millions of events a day.

12
00:00:43.836 --> 00:00:45.860
But each of those measures what arrived.

13
00:00:45.860 --> 00:00:47.460
None of them measures what didn't.

14
00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:50.797
NIST keeps apart two things
that are easy to buy as one.

15
00:00:50.797 --> 00:00:54.060
AU-12 says generate the audit records.

16
00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:58.486
AU-6 says review and analyze
them for unusual activity

17
00:00:58.486 --> 00:01:03.010
and report what you find, and
SI-4 adds detecting attacks.

18
00:01:03.010 --> 00:01:07.485
An agency can satisfy the first
completely and still fail the other two.

19
00:01:07.485 --> 00:01:12.085
OMB M-26-14 measures the same way.

20
00:01:12.085 --> 00:01:16.256
Logs must be searchable for six
months and retrievable for a year,

21
00:01:16.256 --> 00:01:20.814
but its maturity model scores collection
by alerting: under half of the

22
00:01:20.814 --> 00:01:25.835
baseline covered by actionable alerts at
Level 1, at least 70 percent at Level 3.

23
00:01:25.835 --> 00:01:28.963
And Microsoft says logs
ingested only into the

24
00:01:28.963 --> 00:01:32.010
Sentinel data lake tier
don't generate alerts.

25
00:01:32.010 --> 00:01:35.170
That source meets retention
and detects nothing.

26
00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:40.545
Coverage gaps don't announce themselves,
and there are four ways they stay quiet.

27
00:01:40.545 --> 00:01:44.231
Some sources are never connected:
Azure resource logs aren't

28
00:01:44.231 --> 00:01:47.695
collected until each resource
has a diagnostic setting.

29
00:01:47.695 --> 00:01:52.493
Some connected sources aren't flowing:
Microsoft says the AWS connector's

30
00:01:52.493 --> 00:01:56.820
green status means a collection
rule exists, not that data arrived.

31
00:01:56.820 --> 00:01:59.608
Some collected data is never analyzed.

32
00:01:59.608 --> 00:02:02.115
And some sources are
switched off on purpose;

33
00:02:02.115 --> 00:02:06.383
CISA calls tampering with logging
well-documented threat actor behavior.

34
00:02:06.383 --> 00:02:12.068
In the 2023 Exchange Online intrusion, a
federal victim caught it with its own alert

35
00:02:12.068 --> 00:02:17.507
on mailbox-access events that, at the time,
came only with a premium audit license.

36
00:02:17.507 --> 00:02:20.633
Agencies without those events
had nothing to look at.

37
00:02:20.633 --> 00:02:23.203
None of these gaps shows
up in what arrived,

38
00:02:23.203 --> 00:02:26.508
only in comparing what arrived
with what was expected.

39
00:02:26.508 --> 00:02:33.848
That's why M-26-14 tells agencies to use
CDM and asset data to confirm log coverage,

40
00:02:33.848 --> 00:02:39.520
and why BOD 23-01 calls asset
visibility a basic pre-condition.

41
00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:42.190
The inventory is the denominator.

42
00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:49.152
A multi-cloud agency runs several stacks:
Azure Update Manager, AWS Systems Manager,

43
00:02:49.152 --> 00:02:54.553
a virtualization lifecycle manager,
firewall syslog, a Linux host agent.

44
00:02:54.553 --> 00:02:58.940
Five partial views can't prove one
vulnerability is fixed everywhere.

45
00:02:58.940 --> 00:03:01.904
The program's answer is
an evidence contract:

46
00:03:01.904 --> 00:03:07.352
one record shape every source emits, with
eight fields fixed before any data flows.

47
00:03:07.352 --> 00:03:11.776
They carry the six things NIST's
AU-3 asks a record to establish,

48
00:03:11.776 --> 00:03:15.940
plus the control it evidences,
a verdict and a disposition.

49
00:03:15.940 --> 00:03:19.715
The join key is authoritative
asset identity plus time.

50
00:03:19.715 --> 00:03:22.003
Every stack has its own identifier.

51
00:03:22.003 --> 00:03:25.414
Even Microsoft's normalization
model lists separate

52
00:03:25.414 --> 00:03:28.977
device IDs per product and
calls a hostname a last resort.

53
00:03:28.977 --> 00:03:33.402
So the key comes from the naming plane,
which has to exist before the pipeline.

54
00:03:33.402 --> 00:03:35.952
And time means when
the state was observed,

55
00:03:35.952 --> 00:03:39.303
because arrival time joins
the wrong records silently.

56
00:03:39.303 --> 00:03:44.028
One caution: the system-wide,
time-correlated trail,

57
00:03:44.028 --> 00:03:47.066
AU-12(1), is a High-baseline control.

58
00:03:47.066 --> 00:03:50.370
At Moderate, the program
selects it by design.

59
00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:52.608
What must each plane emit?

60
00:03:52.608 --> 00:03:57.104
The program treats the estate
as six planes: identity,

61
00:03:57.104 --> 00:04:02.008
cloud platform, Microsoft 365,
endpoints, network and data.

62
00:04:02.008 --> 00:04:05.055
No one plane covers M-26-14's baseline,

63
00:04:05.055 --> 00:04:10.133
and systems a third party runs
for the agency are in scope too.

64
00:04:10.133 --> 00:04:13.795
Each plane has a default that
looks instrumented and isn't.

65
00:04:13.795 --> 00:04:18.490
Entra ID shows sign-ins but keeps
them only 30 days with P1 or P2,

66
00:04:18.490 --> 00:04:22.079
so the program streams
all four sign-in streams,

67
00:04:22.079 --> 00:04:26.682
not just the interactive one,
plus Microsoft Graph activity.

68
00:04:26.682 --> 00:04:30.743
Azure deletes its activity log
after 90 days, and resource logs

69
00:04:30.743 --> 00:04:35.194
aren't collected at all until each
resource has a diagnostic setting.

70
00:04:35.194 --> 00:04:40.318
Microsoft 365 audit records last
180 days on the Standard tier;

71
00:04:40.318 --> 00:04:45.736
the one-year default applies only
to users with an E5-level license,

72
00:04:45.736 --> 00:04:49.283
everyone else, guests
included, stays at 180.

73
00:04:49.283 --> 00:04:54.158
So emission is enforced by policy, and
verified by a query that names every

74
00:04:54.158 --> 00:04:59.960
expected table, so a missing one shows up
as a row instead of silently disappearing.

75
00:05:00.710 --> 00:05:03.772
A workspace full of tables
is still a warehouse.

76
00:05:03.772 --> 00:05:07.344
Rules, incidents and automation
make it a detection engine,

77
00:05:07.344 --> 00:05:12.082
and the program's rule is strict: no source
counts as monitored until an enabled

78
00:05:12.082 --> 00:05:16.747
rule reads it, and no rule counts until
its output reaches a queue someone owns.

79
00:05:16.747 --> 00:05:19.872
The engine also sets a
floor on time to detect.

80
00:05:19.872 --> 00:05:22.969
A scheduled rule runs
every five minutes at best,

81
00:05:22.969 --> 00:05:25.447
with a five-minute delay for ingestion.

82
00:05:25.447 --> 00:05:29.462
A near-real-time rule runs every
minute on a two-minute delay,

83
00:05:29.462 --> 00:05:32.248
and a workspace can
enable only 50 of them.

84
00:05:32.248 --> 00:05:34.984
Behavioral baselines are slower by design.

85
00:05:34.984 --> 00:05:40.923
Defender XDR detects what its own sensors
see on endpoints, identities and email.

86
00:05:40.923 --> 00:05:47.735
Sentinel rules cover what Defender can't
see: DNS, SASE, SQL audit and other clouds.

87
00:05:47.735 --> 00:05:52.133
Then prepare for the assessor's test:
show an alert from the last 30 days,

88
00:05:52.133 --> 00:05:54.635
the incident it opened and what was done.

89
00:05:54.635 --> 00:06:00.500
That takes four records: the alert, the
ticket, the response notes and the closure.

90
00:06:01.250 --> 00:06:03.700
Two federal clocks run against this work.

91
00:06:03.700 --> 00:06:08.955
CISA's BOD 26-04, issued June 10,
2026, revoked BOD 22-01 and BOD 19-02,

92
00:06:08.955 --> 00:06:15.598
and replaced severity buckets with four
questions about each vulnerable asset:

93
00:06:15.598 --> 00:06:22.439
is it publicly exposed, is the flaw in the
Known Exploited Vulnerabilities catalog,

94
00:06:22.439 --> 00:06:28.388
can exploitation be automated, and
does it give partial or total control?

95
00:06:28.388 --> 00:06:30.826
The answers set a maximum timeline.

96
00:06:30.826 --> 00:06:34.769
Three days, with forensic triage
on the worst rows, because,

97
00:06:34.769 --> 00:06:39.700
in CISA's words, applying a patch
generally does not evict a threat actor.

98
00:06:39.700 --> 00:06:43.762
Then 14 days, 60 days, or
the next system upgrade.

99
00:06:43.762 --> 00:06:48.051
The clock starts when CISA lists
the flaw or when the agency finds

100
00:06:48.051 --> 00:06:52.262
it and updates CDM, whichever comes
first, and there are no waivers.

101
00:06:52.262 --> 00:06:57.174
Phase III, remediation within the
table, applies from December 7.

102
00:06:57.174 --> 00:07:03.625
M-26-14's clock runs from CISA's
Logging Reference Architecture,

103
00:07:03.625 --> 00:07:10.074
published August 20, 2026: the
Agency Logging Plan within 90 days,

104
00:07:10.074 --> 00:07:15.900
and maturity Levels 1, 2 and 3
within 120, 180 and 320 days.

105
00:07:16.650 --> 00:07:22.383
Vulnerability management fails at three
points assessors probe: a stale inventory,

106
00:07:22.383 --> 00:07:26.888
prioritization by score alone, and
no record of found, due and fixed.

107
00:07:26.888 --> 00:07:29.237
The program's answer is a daily queue.

108
00:07:29.237 --> 00:07:31.824
Pull the KEV feed before
the morning review.

109
00:07:31.824 --> 00:07:34.800
Pull findings from Defender
Vulnerability Management.

110
00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:39.575
Join each device to the authoritative
asset record and its exposure tag.

111
00:07:39.575 --> 00:07:44.190
Look up CISA's automation and impact
data, apply the directive's table,

112
00:07:44.190 --> 00:07:47.612
and keep the first-detected
date, never resetting it.

113
00:07:47.612 --> 00:07:49.371
Use the catalog's due date,

114
00:07:49.371 --> 00:07:53.474
because Defender's vulnerability
data carries no KEV flag.

115
00:07:53.474 --> 00:07:57.450
Treat an asset with no exposure
tag as publicly exposed.

116
00:07:57.450 --> 00:08:01.987
And Defender's exports carry no
history, so the program keeps its own.

117
00:08:01.987 --> 00:08:05.225
A scanner authors no
state; it measures a gap.

118
00:08:05.225 --> 00:08:08.346
Each platform patches with
its own native stack,

119
00:08:08.346 --> 00:08:13.262
and every stack returns the same
remediation record, keyed to the same asset.

120
00:08:13.262 --> 00:08:18.270
A finding closes when the fix is recorded
and the next scan no longer sees it.

121
00:08:19.020 --> 00:08:21.045
Three more streams share the queue.

122
00:08:21.045 --> 00:08:26.383
Configuration first: a patched host that
drifts from its baseline isn't remediated.

123
00:08:26.383 --> 00:08:30.244
Baselines come from
SCuBA, CIS or DISA STIGs.

124
00:08:30.244 --> 00:08:35.380
Azure machine configuration's auto-correct
mode fixes drift without recording it,

125
00:08:35.380 --> 00:08:38.133
so the program keeps it
for low-risk settings.

126
00:08:38.133 --> 00:08:39.571
Cloud posture next.

127
00:08:39.571 --> 00:08:43.085
A storage account open to the
internet has no missing patch,

128
00:08:43.085 --> 00:08:44.807
so a scanner never reports it.

129
00:08:44.807 --> 00:08:48.807
Each provider's native posture tool
is primary for its own platform.

130
00:08:48.807 --> 00:08:51.939
Defender for Cloud doesn't
cover Oracle Cloud,

131
00:08:51.939 --> 00:08:55.895
and its AWS connector isn't
available in Azure Government.

132
00:08:55.895 --> 00:08:56.807
Then data.

133
00:08:56.807 --> 00:09:00.446
The CUI framework has two
levels, Basic and Specified,

134
00:09:00.446 --> 00:09:03.506
decided by the authority
in the CUI Registry:

135
00:09:03.506 --> 00:09:08.882
Privacy Act records are Basic, and
federal taxpayer information is Specified.

136
00:09:08.882 --> 00:09:13.945
Labels drive data loss prevention on
the service, the endpoint and the CASB.

137
00:09:13.945 --> 00:09:15.395
And check queue membership.

138
00:09:15.395 --> 00:09:20.009
Since early October 2026, a built-in
Microsoft tuning rule keeps DLP

139
00:09:20.009 --> 00:09:25.220
signals out of the incident queue
unless an administrator turns it off.

140
00:09:25.970 --> 00:09:27.983
Who runs security operations?

141
00:09:27.983 --> 00:09:30.057
Most agencies can name their SOC.

142
00:09:30.057 --> 00:09:34.668
Fewer can say who decided that a log
source is collected, how long it's kept,

143
00:09:34.668 --> 00:09:38.357
or who accepted the risk when
it went quiet for three weeks.

144
00:09:38.357 --> 00:09:39.846
The program assigns them.

145
00:09:39.846 --> 00:09:44.582
The CIO sets scope and funding
and designates the top-level SOC.

146
00:09:44.582 --> 00:09:49.392
The CISO decides what each plane must
emit, owns the Agency Logging Plan,

147
00:09:49.392 --> 00:09:52.758
and approves any playbook
that acts on production.

148
00:09:52.758 --> 00:09:55.432
Platform owners keep
their own planes emitting.

149
00:09:55.432 --> 00:09:58.611
Security engineering builds
the SIEM, the rules,

150
00:09:58.611 --> 00:10:01.382
the playbooks and the evidence pipeline.

151
00:10:01.382 --> 00:10:05.707
The SOC runs the queue, and CISA's
one-hour clock starts there.

152
00:10:05.707 --> 00:10:07.621
Two roles are easy to forget.

153
00:10:07.621 --> 00:10:11.961
The records officer decides disposition,
and must concur before anyone

154
00:10:11.961 --> 00:10:15.795
shortens retention, because evidence
that's gone can't come back.

155
00:10:15.795 --> 00:10:20.307
And the authorizing official, the
only official who can accept risk,

156
00:10:20.307 --> 00:10:24.497
accepts the risk of not seeing:
a gap carried rather than closed,

157
00:10:24.497 --> 00:10:28.070
a suppression with no end
date, a missed deadline.

158
00:10:28.820 --> 00:10:30.433
How do detections get built?

159
00:10:30.433 --> 00:10:31.358
As code.

160
00:10:31.358 --> 00:10:34.084
The rule library lives in version control,

161
00:10:34.084 --> 00:10:39.070
and nobody edits a production rule in the
portal; a change is a reviewed commit.

162
00:10:39.070 --> 00:10:43.432
Every rule has one named owner,
a test and a review date.

163
00:10:43.432 --> 00:10:45.870
Each rule moves through five steps.

164
00:10:45.870 --> 00:10:49.620
Author it, starting from Microsoft's
template where one exists.

165
00:10:49.620 --> 00:10:53.068
Test it against history and
replay a known-bad sample,

166
00:10:53.068 --> 00:10:56.595
because a rule that can't be
shown to fire isn't finished.

167
00:10:56.595 --> 00:10:58.955
Run it alert-only for at least two weeks,

168
00:10:58.955 --> 00:11:01.757
and until its false
positives are documented.

169
00:11:01.757 --> 00:11:02.620
Promote it.

170
00:11:02.620 --> 00:11:04.520
Then review it at least quarterly.

171
00:11:04.520 --> 00:11:08.970
Where a vendor analytic isn't available
in the agency's cloud, the program

172
00:11:08.970 --> 00:11:13.345
rebuilds an equivalent inside its own
boundary, and says it isn't parity.

173
00:11:13.345 --> 00:11:14.407
Plan for rot.

174
00:11:14.407 --> 00:11:17.548
Thresholds drift, sources
stop without an error,

175
00:11:17.548 --> 00:11:22.808
and Sentinel disables a rule after repeated
failures and marks it auto disabled.

176
00:11:22.808 --> 00:11:26.960
The standing tuning effort is the
real cost of having a library.

177
00:11:27.710 --> 00:11:32.348
Detection without response shortens
the investigation, not the exposure.

178
00:11:32.348 --> 00:11:37.345
So every playbook is confidence-gated:
high confidence prepares containment,

179
00:11:37.345 --> 00:11:42.573
medium confidence enriches and tickets for
a human, and low confidence never acts.

180
00:11:42.573 --> 00:11:45.647
Responses sit on the
workbook's actuation ladder.

181
00:11:45.647 --> 00:11:46.960
L1 observes.

182
00:11:46.960 --> 00:11:48.260
L2 advises.

183
00:11:48.260 --> 00:11:53.103
L3, gated actuation, is the default
for anything with a large blast radius:

184
00:11:53.103 --> 00:11:57.381
the playbook checks its preconditions
and prepares the action,

185
00:11:57.381 --> 00:11:59.560
and a named approver releases it.

186
00:11:59.560 --> 00:12:02.194
L4 runs alone, logged and reversible,

187
00:12:02.194 --> 00:12:06.847
for enumerated classes such as
closing known-benign incidents.

188
00:12:06.847 --> 00:12:10.906
Three classes stay at L3
permanently: isolating a server,

189
00:12:10.906 --> 00:12:16.086
disabling a privileged or emergency
account, and any tenant-wide change.

190
00:12:16.086 --> 00:12:19.531
Defender XDR's attack
disruption acts on its own,

191
00:12:19.531 --> 00:12:24.010
so the authorizing official
decides whether to accept it as L4.

192
00:12:24.010 --> 00:12:25.472
Latency is real.

193
00:12:25.472 --> 00:12:30.640
Session revocation can take up to 15
minutes in services that support continuous

194
00:12:30.640 --> 00:12:35.503
access evaluation, and a hybrid user must
be disabled in Active Directory too,

195
00:12:35.503 --> 00:12:38.300
or the next synchronization undoes it.

196
00:12:39.050 --> 00:12:40.562
Last, prove it works.

197
00:12:40.562 --> 00:12:44.823
Continuous monitoring is a
pipeline: emit, normalize,

198
00:12:44.823 --> 00:12:49.762
correlate, bind each record to
its control, report, and respond.

199
00:12:49.762 --> 00:12:52.187
The metrics are built
so they can't flatter.

200
00:12:52.187 --> 00:12:57.959
Detection coverage counts how many of
M-26-14's eleven baseline activities

201
00:12:57.959 --> 00:13:03.450
have an enabled, tested rule over a
fresh source, not how many rules exist.

202
00:13:03.450 --> 00:13:09.288
Telemetry completeness treats a stale
required source as an AU-5 failure.

203
00:13:09.288 --> 00:13:15.388
KEV remediation counts closures within
the BOD 26-04 timeline, plus open items

204
00:13:15.388 --> 00:13:21.675
past it, because a share of the open queue
improves every time new findings arrive.

205
00:13:21.675 --> 00:13:26.337
And configuration reports passing,
failing and excepted settings separately.

206
00:13:26.337 --> 00:13:28.224
Read outside scores carefully.

207
00:13:28.224 --> 00:13:31.652
M-26-14 scores each system
at its lowest element,

208
00:13:31.652 --> 00:13:37.275
and a system can reach Advanced while
missing the six-month searchable floor.

209
00:13:37.275 --> 00:13:41.616
FedRAMP's 46 Key Security
Indicators bind cloud providers,

210
00:13:41.616 --> 00:13:46.488
not agencies; the agency reviews
each provider's quarterly report.

211
00:13:46.488 --> 00:13:48.846
A finding closes on re-test evidence,

212
00:13:48.846 --> 00:13:52.810
or the authorizing official
carries it with an end date.

213
00:13:53.560 --> 00:13:54.935
So, four decisions.

214
00:13:54.935 --> 00:13:57.994
Name the planes and assets
that are expected to emit,

215
00:13:57.994 --> 00:14:00.910
and check each one against
what actually arrives.

216
00:14:00.910 --> 00:14:04.318
Stand up the naming plane
before the evidence pipeline,

217
00:14:04.318 --> 00:14:06.985
so every record joins
on one asset identity.

218
00:14:06.985 --> 00:14:11.226
Fund the Agency Logging Plan as
the program's visibility plan,

219
00:14:11.226 --> 00:14:12.972
not as a storage purchase.

220
00:14:12.972 --> 00:14:18.516
And send every blind spot to the
authorizing official: a telemetry gap,

221
00:14:18.516 --> 00:14:24.634
a suppression with no end date, a missed
BOD 26-04 deadline, each with an end date.

222
00:14:24.634 --> 00:14:26.835
The next step needs no procurement.

223
00:14:26.835 --> 00:14:29.743
Run the coverage check
against the inventory,

224
00:14:29.743 --> 00:14:34.334
tag the exposure of every externally
reachable asset before December 7,

225
00:14:34.334 --> 00:14:38.160
and count a source as covered
only when a tested rule reads it.

226
00:14:38.160 --> 00:14:40.323
You can't defend what you can't see,

227
00:14:40.323 --> 00:14:44.248
and a finding closes only when
the thing is fixed, with proof.

228
00:14:44.248 --> 00:14:50.122
The Federal Modernization Workbook's Book
12 has the detail, every claim footnoted.

229
00:14:50.122 --> 00:14:50.940
Thank you.
